{"name":"Golden Hamster","hex":"#da9e38","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden hamster","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small rodent of species Mesocricetus auratus, popular as a pet and used for scientific research."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden_hamster"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden hamster","blocks":[{"text":"The golden hamster or Syrian hamster is a rodent belonging to the hamster subfamily, Cricetinae. Their natural geographical range is in an arid region of northern Syria and southern Turkey. Their numbers have been declining in the wild due to a loss of habitat from agriculture and deliberate elimination by humans. Thus, wild golden hamsters are now considered endangered by the International Union for Conservation of Nature. However, captive breeding programs are well established, and captive-bred golden hamsters are often kept as small house pets. They are also used as scientific research animals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Golden_hamster_front_1.jpg/330px-Golden_hamster_front_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_hamster","wikidata":"Q204175"},{"title":"Hamster","blocks":[{"text":"Hamsters are rodents belonging to the subfamily Cricetinae, which contains 19 species classified in seven genera. They have become established as popular small pets. The best-known species of hamster is the golden or Syrian hamster, which is the type most commonly kept as a pet. Other hamster species commonly kept as pets are the three species of dwarf hamster, Campbell's dwarf hamster, the winter white dwarf hamster and the Roborovski hamster, and the less common Chinese hamster."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/European_hamster_%28Cricetus_cricetus%29_Meidling.jpg/330px-European_hamster_%28Cricetus_cricetus%29_Meidling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamster","wikidata":"Q6573"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Mesocricetus_auratus_02_MWNH_1067.jpg/500px-Mesocricetus_auratus_02_MWNH_1067.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Mesocricetus_auratus_02_MWNH_1067.jpg/1280px-Mesocricetus_auratus_02_MWNH_1067.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Mesocricetus_auratus_02_MWNH_1067.jpg/500px-Mesocricetus_auratus_02_MWNH_1067.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Skull of a golden hamster","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMesocricetus_auratus_02_MWNH_1067.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/GoldenHamster.jpg/500px-GoldenHamster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/GoldenHamster.jpg/1280px-GoldenHamster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/GoldenHamster.jpg/500px-GoldenHamster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Male golden hamster standing up","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoldenHamster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Cheeks.JPG/500px-Cheeks.